# Simplicity Rules

This repository values practical engineering over theoretical engineering. It is maintained by a small team working closely with AI. Optimize for readability, maintainability, and low cognitive load.

## Before Adding Anything

1. Every new folder, file, abstraction, generic, and helper has a maintenance cost. Justify it or don't add it.

2. When multiple solutions exist, choose the one a new engineer understands immediately.

3. If unsure, choose the simpler solution. Small understandable improvements beat large rewrites.

## Do NOT

4. Do NOT create one-function or one-class wrapper files. Inline or colocate small helpers.

5. Do NOT split a file just because it is long. Prefer cohesive files. Split only along a real domain seam.

6. Do NOT create `shared` / `common` / `base` / `core` / `abstract` folders without strong justification.

7. Do NOT introduce Clean Architecture, CQRS, Factory patterns, or extra Repository layers beyond NestJS default DI + TypeORM.

8. Do NOT add abstractions for only two similar functions. Wait for meaningful duplication.

9. Do NOT hide business logic behind indirection. It must be traceable in one read.

10. Do NOT refactor simply because something can be refactored. Preserve business correctness above all.

## Do

11. Prefer explicit, readable code over clever code.

12. Reuse existing services, hooks, and utilities before writing new ones — search first.

13. Keep the abstraction level flat. NestJS module → service → repository is the whole backend stack. Do not add layers.

14. Make abstractions only when they eliminate meaningful duplication (rule of thumb: 5+ occurrences, not 2).

## Recommending Changes

15. When recommending a change, explain the trade-off, the maintenance impact, and why it is worthwhile for THIS project — not generic best practice.

16. Prefer recommending simplifications (removing complexity) over recommending additions.

## Known Complexity Hotspots (be careful, do not split reactively)

- `backend/src/marketplace/market-data/cardhedger-pricing.service.ts` (~1960 lines)
- `backend/src/psa/psa.service.ts` (~1710 lines)
- `backend/src/psa/psa-public-api.service.ts` (~1520 lines)
- `backend/src/marketplace/collections/collection-market.service.ts` (~1400 lines)
- `backend/src/marketplace/market-data/cardhedger-resolve.service.ts` (~1360 lines)

These are cohesive complexity concentrations. Split only when a specific change reveals a natural seam — never reactively.
